# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: vle-vector-tolerance-undefined-variable — forward-reference compile warning in builtin-vle-g-vectol
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-vle-vector-tolerance-undefined-variable

* Context

A from-scratch SBCL compile of =clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core= prints:

: caught WARNING:
:   undefined variable: CLAUTOLISP.AUTOLISP-BUILTINS-CORE::*VLE-VECTOR-TOLERANCE*
:   (BLOCK ...BUILTIN-VLE-G-VECTOL ...*VLE-VECTOR-TOLERANCE*)

Found 2026-08-02 while fixing [[reactor-builtins-undefined-functions]]
(same "scrolls past in a green run" class, milder variant).

* Problem

Unlike the reactor case, this is NOT a runtime bug: the special variable
is defined in the same file/package, only *after* its first use —

- used at =autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp:6843= (inside
  =builtin-vle-g-vectol=),
- defined at =api.lisp:6845= =(defparameter *vle-vector-tolerance* 1.0d-10 …)=.

So the compiler warns about a forward reference; at run time the
=defparameter= has already executed at load, so the variable is bound
and the builtin works. Purely a compile-time warning / ordering nit.

* Proposal

Move the =(defparameter *vle-vector-tolerance* …)= (and its companion
doc, api.lisp:6845/7283) *above* its first use (=builtin-vle-g-vectol=,
~6840), so a from-scratch compile is clean. No behaviour change.

This supports the broader goal noted in
[[reactor-builtins-undefined-functions]]: a CI guard that fails the
build on any "undefined function/variable" in a from-scratch compile —
which requires the tree to be warning-clean first.

* Acceptance criteria

- From-scratch =make -C clautolisp test-sbcl
  TEST_SYSTEM=clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/tests= compiles with no
  =undefined variable *VLE-VECTOR-TOLERANCE*= warning.
- builtins-core suite stays green; VLE_G_VECTOL / vector builtins
  unchanged.
